# No search spam

Google's spam policies apply to classic results and to generative features
that read the same index. Other engines punish the same junk.

**Never:**

- Keyword stuffing, hidden text, cloaking, sneaky redirects, doorway pages
- Scaled content: many thin URLs, fan-out variants, or AI drafts whose
  purpose is ranking or citation manipulation
- A second "AI version" of the same page, or chunking a page into AI-bait
  fragments
- Structured data that does not match visible content (fake FAQ, reviews,
  ratings, breadcrumbs, Organization)
- Inauthentic mentions: bought comments, fake Reddit/Wikipedia, review spam
- Expired-domain, site-reputation, or link-spam schemes
- `noindex` / robots Disallow games to hide thin copies while ranking a
  different URL
- Promising a ranking, snippet, AI Overview, or citation as a deliverable

**Do instead:** one honest page per topic, crawlable HTML, schema that
mirrors the page, sources you can defend. If the user asks for a banned
tactic, refuse and use `optimizing-for-discovery` / `writing-citable-content`.
